  {
   "id": 1,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 20,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "THALAMOCORTICAL LOOP. A relay nucleus and a cortical patch wired in both directions through a fibre tract drawn as a cylinder. Every synapse is local, so the latency around the loop is the tract crossed in hops rather than one long axon: the connect radius is 240 um against a 1150 um separation, and no single synapse spans it. That is how the tract length reaches the timing, and it is worth being exact about, because the note here used to claim the delays came from one axon over a real distance and they never did.",
    "width": 340
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 2,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 170,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "Thalamocortical relay cells use the TC preset, which sits just above its own resting fixed point: with no input it fires on its own. That is how the published model uses it, since a rebound burst has to rebound from somewhere, so a holding current keeps it below threshold until something drives it.",
    "width": 340
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
